  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">GenDev: Advancing Women for Resilient Agricultural Supply Chains (AWRAS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Bureau for Inclusive Growth, Partnerships, and Innovation (IPI)):  The Advancing Women for Resilient Agricultural Supply Chains (AWRAS) is a public-private partnership aimed at facilitating a common understanding of market-based, core business solutions that advance women&apos;s equal access to, and control over resources, and opportunities, and decision-making authority in agricultural supply chains as a core strategy for achieving sustainability goals. AWRAS builds on lessons learned from USAID&apos;s partnership with PepsiCo under the and the Investing in Women to Strengthen Supply Chains Global Development Alliance to deepen our continued work with PepsiCo to embed women&apos;s empowerment approaches into their core business and bring a successful approach to additional corporate partners. Private sector partners include PepsiCo, Unilever, Danone, McCormick &amp; Co, and Nespresso. With FY24 funding, AWRAS interventions will include: mapping, assessing, and analyzing existing and planned corporate sustainability and social impact strategies, programs, and stakeholders; designing, testing, and piloting WEE interventions that strengthen corporate partners&apos; approach to achieving sustainable supply chains; leveraging activity data to provide MEL support to enable USAID and its private sector partners to assess the impact of WEE initiatives, monitor supply chain traceability, and evaluate the approaches effectiveness; and supporting the integration of WEE approaches across corporate policies, practices, strategies, and staffing as a core part of business operations in their supply chains, including through their work with aggregators and other supply chain stakeholders. These interventions contribute to the expected result that corporate partners will change the way they do business and reach sustainability goals by integrating women&apos;s economic empowerment approaches throughout their agricultural supply chains and make the business case for these approaches that allow them to co</narrative>
  </description>
